["flow d68c82 handover: A handover carries judgment, not what can be looked up (2026-10-13)" "The idea would be that the flow could create its handover although we have to train the machine properly on how this handover is composed. It shouldn't focus on the technical or on describing things that can be re-verified, as in, \"such revision was committed and pushed.\" All of this narration of chronology and stuff, I don't want that because it's not a judgment call. There's no need for the machine to preoccupy itself with using its power to compose something that the next flow can just look up, which it'll never actually depend on interpretation.\n\nI don't need all of this describing things that require no judgment. I want to maximize the inference/judgment aspect of the machine and not use it for something which a well-written tool could just acquire in a single call with a handful of tokens on a command call (which would return all of this information almost instantly and reliably). Not that we have to write these tools. I just don't want to use the judgment of machines for that."]
